## Solution

**Claim.** Let $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^n$ be a bounded smooth domain. A function $u\in W^{1,p}(\Omega;\mathbb{R}^n)$ admits a continuous representative (indeed a Hölder-continuous one) **if and only if $p>n$** (with the convention that for $n=1$ the threshold $p>1$ is the Morrey range, while $p=1$ also gives continuity in one dimension by absolute continuity; the clean Sobolev–Morrey threshold valid for all $n\ge 1$ is $p>n$).

Below is the proof of the embedding $W^{1,p}(\Omega)\hookrightarrow C^{0,\alpha}(\overline{\Omega})$ for $p>n$, $\alpha=1-n/p$, together with the sharpness: $p=n$ fails for $n\ge 2$.

---

### 1. Reduction to the scalar case

$W^{1,p}(\Omega;\mathbb{R}^n)=W^{1,p}(\Omega)^n$ componentwise, and a vector-valued map is continuous iff each component is. So it suffices to prove the scalar statement.

### 2. Morrey's inequality (interior estimate)

Let $u\in W^{1,p}(\Omega)$ with $p>n$, and let $B_R(x)\subset\Omega$ be a ball. By the Poincaré–Sobolev inequality on balls (mean-value form),

$$
\int_{B_R(x)}|u(y)-u_{B_R(x)}|^p\,dy \;\le\; C\,R^p\int_{B_R(x)}|\nabla u(y)|^p\,dy,
$$

where $u_{B_R(x)}=\frac{1}{|B_R|}\int_{B_R}u$. By Hölder,

$$
\int_{B_R(x)}|\nabla u|^p \le \|\nabla u\|_{L^p(\Omega)}^p .
$$

Combining and using $|B_R|=\omega_n R^n$,

$$
\left(\frac{1}{|B_R|}\int_{B_R}|u-u_{B_R}|^p\right)^{1/p}
\le C\,R\left(\frac{1}{|B_R|}\int_{B_R}|\nabla u|^p\right)^{1/p}
\le C\,R\,\omega_n^{1/p-1}\,R^{-n/p}\|\nabla u\|_{L^p}
= C\,R^{1-n/p}\|\nabla u\|_{L^p}.
$$

A standard Campanato argument (or the direct Morrey lemma) now shows that $u$ has a Hölder-continuous representative with exponent $\alpha=1-n/p>0$ and

$$
|u(x)-u(y)|\le C\,\|\nabla u\|_{L^p(\Omega)}\,|x-y|^{1-n/p},\qquad x,y\in\Omega',
$$

for every $\Omega'\Subset\Omega$. The key point: the oscillation on a ball of radius $R$ is controlled by $R^{1-n/p}$, and $1-n/p>0$ precisely because **$p>n$**.

### 3. Extension to the boundary and global continuity

Since $\Omega$ is smooth (hence Lipschitz), the Calderón–Zygmund/Stein extension operator $E:W^{1,p}(\Omega)\to W^{1,p}(\mathbb{R}^n)$ is bounded for all $1\le p\le\infty$. Applying the interior Morrey estimate to $Eu$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$ and restricting back to $\overline{\Omega}$ gives

$$
|u(x)-u(y)|\le C\,\|u\|_{W^{1,p}(\Omega)}\,|x-y|^{1-n/p},\qquad x,y\in\overline{\Omega}.
$$

Thus $u$ has a representative in $C^{0,\alpha}(\overline{\Omega})$, $\alpha=1-n/p$, with the continuous embedding

$$
W^{1,p}(\Omega)\hookrightarrow C^{0,\alpha}(\overline{\Omega}),\qquad p>n,\;\;\alpha=1-\frac{n}{p}.
$$

In particular $u$ has a continuous representative.

### 4. Sharpness: $p=n$ fails (for $n\ge 2$)

Take $\Omega=B_{1/2}(0)\subset\mathbb{R}^n$, $n\ge 2$, and

$$
u(x)=\log\log\frac{1}{|x|}\;\chi_{\{|x|<1/2\}} \quad\text{(smoothly cut off near $|x|=1/2$)}.
$$

A direct computation in polar coordinates gives, near $0$,

$$
|\nabla u(x)|=\frac{1}{|x|\log(1/|x|)},
\qquad
\int_{B_\varepsilon}|\nabla u|^n\,dx
=\omega_{n-1}\int_0^\varepsilon\frac{r^{n-1}}{r^n\log^n(1/r)}\,dr
=\omega_{n-1}\int_0^\varepsilon\frac{dr}{r\,\log^n(1/r)}.
$$

For $n\ge 2$, $\int_0^{1/2}\frac{dr}{r\log^n(1/r)}<\infty$ (substitution $s=\log(1/r)$ gives $\int_{\log 2}^{\infty} s^{-n}\,ds<\infty$ since $n\ge 2$). So $\nabla u\in L^n$, and clearly $u\in L^n$, hence $u\in W^{1,n}(\Omega)$. But $u(x)\to+\infty$ as $x\to 0$, so $u$ has **no** continuous representative. Therefore $W^{1,n}\not\hookrightarrow C^0$ for $n\ge 2$.

For $p<n$ the failure is even more severe: $W^{1,p}\not\hookrightarrow L^\infty$ (e.g. $u(x)=|x|^{-\beta}$ with $\beta=(n-p)/p$), so a fortiori no continuous representative.

### 5. The case $n=1$

When $n=1$, every $u\in W^{1,1}((a,b))$ has an absolutely continuous representative, hence a continuous one. The Morrey threshold $p>n=1$ (i.e. $p>1$) is the range giving Hölder continuity; $p=1$ gives plain continuity. The uniform statement "$p>n$" captures the Hölder-continuous regime across all dimensions, and is the standard sharp Sobolev–Morrey threshold.

---

### Conclusion

For a bounded smooth domain $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^n$, functions in $W^{1,p}(\Omega;\mathbb{R}^n)$ possess a continuous representative precisely in the **Morrey range**

$$
\boxed{\,p>n\,}
$$

(with the embedding $W^{1,p}(\Omega;\mathbb{R}^n)\hookrightarrow C^{0,\,1-n/p}(\overline{\Omega};\mathbb{R}^n)$, and the threshold being sharp for $n\ge 2$).

